  ('math.psda.claims', 'math.psda.claims.design', 'medium', 0, 'mcq', null, 'Researchers recruited volunteers who answered an online ad and randomly assigned half to use a sleep-tracking device and half not to. The group using a sleep-tracking device showed significantly better hours of sleep. Which conclusion is best supported?', '[{"id":"A","text":"A sleep-tracking device likely causes improved hours of sleep for people like the volunteers who answered an online ad."},{"id":"B","text":"A sleep-tracking device causes improved hours of sleep for everyone."},{"id":"C","text":"There is an association, but no conclusion about cause can be drawn."},{"id":"D","text":"No conclusion can be drawn because the groups were chosen at random."}]'::jsonb, '{"choice":"A"}'::jsonb, 'Random assignment supports a cause-and-effect conclusion, but because the participants were not randomly selected from a larger population, the conclusion applies only to people like the participants.', '{"B":"The participants were not a random sample of everyone.","C":"Random assignment to groups is what allows a cause-and-effect conclusion.","D":"Random assignment strengthens, not weakens, the conclusion."}'::jsonb),
  ('math.psda.claims', 'math.psda.claims.design', 'hard', 1, 'mcq', null, 'An observational study of a large random sample of adults found that those who play video games often were more likely to have faster reaction times. Participants chose their own habits; none were assigned. Which statement is best supported?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Choosing to play video games often causes people to have faster reaction times."},{"id":"B","text":"There is no relationship between whether people play video games often and whether they have faster reaction times."},{"id":"C","text":"Everyone who chose to play video games often will have faster reaction times."},{"id":"D","text":"People who play video games often tend to have faster reaction times, but the study does not show that one causes the other."}]'::jsonb, '{"choice":"D"}'::jsonb, 'A random sample supports generalizing the association to the population, but because habits were not randomly assigned, the study cannot show cause and effect.', '{"A":"Without random assignment, other factors could explain the link.","B":"The study did find an association.","C":"An association describes a tendency, not a certainty for every person."}'::jsonb),
